  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One outer electron above a noble-gas core is lost readily, giving the Na⁺ ion.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Potassium is the furthest down Group 1 among these metals, so it loses its outer electron most easily.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A larger atom with more inner shells holds its outer electron less tightly, so ionisation energy falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The alkali metals are soft enough to cut with a knife; they are not hard.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The oil excludes air and moisture, so the metal does not tarnish or react.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂ at steam temperature; in cold water only a thin coat of hydroxide forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The flame colour is that of potassium, lilac; sodium gives a golden yellow flame under the same conditions.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Calcium is above hydrogen but below sodium, so it reacts steadily rather than violently.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂; the metal takes the oxygen and releases the hydrogen.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two outer electrons are lost to give a 2+ ion, as in Mg²⁺ and Ca²⁺.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Magnesium is at the top of the group and reacts with steam, not with cold water to any useful extent.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Magnesium hydroxide is almost insoluble, while barium hydroxide is soluble — the hydroxides become more soluble down the group.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Barium sulphate is effectively insoluble, which is why it is used as a barium meal.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Insoluble barium sulphate passes through the gut without releasing toxic barium ions into the blood.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sodium gives the intense golden yellow that also colours sodium street lamps.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Potassium gives a pale lilac flame, which a trace of sodium can mask.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Calcium gives a brick-red flame; strontium is crimson and barium apple green.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Barium gives an apple-green flame, and its salts are used to colour fireworks green.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lithium gives a crimson flame, the deepest red of the Group 1 metals.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Copper compounds give a blue-green flame, which is why copper roofs turn green as they corrode.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Barium carbonate needs a far higher temperature to decompose than magnesium carbonate.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Stability increases down the group, so the carbonate at the top, magnesium carbonate, decomposes most readily.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lithium carbonate behaves like a Group 2 carbonate and decomposes to the oxide and carbon dioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Group 1 nitrates give the nitrite and oxygen; only lithium nitrate gives the oxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Group 2 nitrates decompose to the oxide, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH; the oxide is basic and gives a strong alkali.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sodium peroxide hydrolyses to sodium hydroxide and hydrogen peroxide, which then decomposes to water and oxygen.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aluminium oxide reacts with both acids and alkalis; the other three are basic oxides.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Calcium oxide is a base: it neutralises the acid in the soil and is cheap enough to spread in tonnes.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Slaked lime is calcium hydroxide, made by adding water to quicklime, CaO.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Quicklime is calcium oxide, CaO, obtained by heating limestone.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Limestone is calcium carbonate, CaCO₃; chalk and marble are other forms of the same compound.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Gypsum is hydrated calcium sulphate; when partly dehydrated it becomes plaster of Paris.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Epsom salt is hydrated magnesium sulphate, used as a laxative and in bath salts.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It neutralises the acid slowly, and its low solubility means it is not absorbed in harmful amounts.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Boiling a fat or oil with sodium hydroxide is saponification, the reaction that makes soap.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It decomposes on heating to release carbon dioxide, which raises the dough; it is also a mild antacid.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n(Na) = 0.10 mol, and 2Na gives 1H₂, so n(H₂) = 0.050 mol and V = 0.050 × 22.4 = 1.12 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n(CaCO₃) = 250 ÷ 100 = 2.50 mol, and the ratio is 1 : 1, so m(CaO) = 2.50 × 56 = 140 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n(Na₂O) = 6.2 ÷ 62 = 0.10 mol, and the ratio is 1 : 2, so n(NaOH) = 0.20 mol and m = 8.0 g.
  ]

